1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,900
In 1977, you could fly from London to
New York in about three and a half hours.

2
00:00:06,517 --> 00:00:09,537
Today, the same trip
takes around seven or eight.

3
00:00:09,855 --> 00:00:12,775
And that isn't just the story
of one unusual plane.

4
00:00:13,455 --> 00:00:15,615
The ordinary jets got stuck too.

5
00:00:16,025 --> 00:00:19,945
The Boeing 747 entered service in 1970.

6
00:00:20,645 --> 00:00:24,165
It cruised at about eighty-five percent
of the speed of sound.

7
00:00:24,848 --> 00:00:27,488
The newest airliners flying today
cruise at...

8
00:00:27,568 --> 00:00:30,588
about eighty-five percent
of the speed of sound.

9
00:00:31,259 --> 00:00:35,959
In those fifty-odd years, computers went
from filling rooms to fitting in pockets.

10
00:00:36,559 --> 00:00:39,699
The speed of a passenger jet barely moved.

11
00:00:40,338 --> 00:00:41,598
So what happened?

12
00:00:42,038 --> 00:00:44,178
Did we forget how to build fast planes?

13
00:00:45,087 --> 00:00:46,487
No. We hit a wall.

14
00:00:46,567 --> 00:00:48,507
And the wall is made of air.

15
00:00:50,717 --> 00:00:54,657
A wing works partly by speeding up the air
that flows over its top.

16
00:00:55,437 --> 00:00:57,917
That air moves faster
than the plane itself.

17
00:00:58,287 --> 00:01:00,547
So when the plane flies
at about eighty percent

18
00:01:00,627 --> 00:01:03,967
of the speed of sound,
parts of the air on top of the wing

19
00:01:04,197 --> 00:01:06,567
are already going faster than sound.

20
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:11,700
And where that supersonic air has to slow
down again, it forms a shock wave.

21
00:01:12,372 --> 00:01:14,052
Shock waves are expensive.

22
00:01:14,872 --> 00:01:18,212
They add a whole new kind of drag,
and it doesn't creep up gently.

23
00:01:18,892 --> 00:01:21,412
Past a certain speed, it shoots up.

24
00:01:21,965 --> 00:01:25,005
For many airliners,
that steep rise begins somewhere

25
00:01:25,235 --> 00:01:28,665
between eighty and eighty-five percent
of the speed of sound.

26
00:01:29,272 --> 00:01:32,312
Just where the 747 flew in 1970.

27
00:01:32,392 --> 00:01:35,172
And just where airliners fly now.

28
00:01:37,162 --> 00:01:39,862
And drag isn't just a number
in a wind tunnel.

29
00:01:40,182 --> 00:01:41,902
It's paid for in fuel.

30
00:01:42,657 --> 00:01:45,537
More drag means the engines
have to push harder.

31
00:01:46,057 --> 00:01:49,157
Pushing harder means burning more fuel
for every kilometre.

32
00:01:49,986 --> 00:01:53,226
And fuel is one of the biggest bills
an airline pays.

33
00:01:54,066 --> 00:01:56,706
Often a quarter to a third
of everything it spends.

34
00:01:57,308 --> 00:02:01,208
So airlines don't simply fly
as fast as their planes can go.

35
00:02:01,628 --> 00:02:06,128
They choose a speed that balances
the cost of fuel against the cost of time.

36
00:02:06,686 --> 00:02:11,566
Too slow, and the crew is paid for longer,
and the plane fits fewer trips into a day.

37
00:02:12,201 --> 00:02:14,981
Too fast, and fuel costs climb sharply.

38
00:02:15,121 --> 00:02:17,261
Airlines look for the balance.

39
00:02:18,059 --> 00:02:23,139
Even some versions of the Boeing 707,
back in the 1960s,

40
00:02:23,219 --> 00:02:26,379
could fly at up to ninety percent
of the speed of sound.

41
00:02:27,019 --> 00:02:29,259
Airlines usually flew it
at about eighty-two.

42
00:02:31,930 --> 00:02:34,530
Engineers did find ways
to push the wall back a little.

43
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:37,130
Swept-back wings.

44
00:02:37,930 --> 00:02:42,430
And in the 1960s, at NASA,
Richard Whitcomb's supercritical wing.

45
00:02:43,017 --> 00:02:45,117
It could have been used to fly faster.

46
00:02:45,857 --> 00:02:49,757
Mostly, airliners used it
to fly at the same speed on less fuel.

47
00:02:50,432 --> 00:02:54,192
Remember that choice.
It's going to keep coming back.

48
00:02:55,461 --> 00:02:59,161
Because somebody did try
to fly faster, right up at the wall.

49
00:03:00,201 --> 00:03:06,021
In 1962, the Convair 990 entered service
as the fastest airliner in the world.

50
00:03:06,331 --> 00:03:09,231
But it never quite reached the speed
it had been promised.

51
00:03:10,031 --> 00:03:13,431
And it burned more fuel
than the Boeing 707.

52
00:03:13,981 --> 00:03:17,441
For the airlines, a little extra speed
wasn't worth the fuel.

53
00:03:18,141 --> 00:03:20,301
Only thirty-seven were ever built.

54
00:03:21,911 --> 00:03:25,271
So flying just a little faster
at the wall doesn't pay.

55
00:03:25,571 --> 00:03:28,091
But what about going straight through it?

56
00:03:29,243 --> 00:03:30,703
That's Concorde.

57
00:03:30,863 --> 00:03:35,503
It cruised at twice the speed of sound,
over two thousand kilometres an hour.

58
00:03:36,061 --> 00:03:38,921
Getting through the wall
didn't make the wall go away.

59
00:03:39,361 --> 00:03:41,321
It just meant paying for it.

60
00:03:41,877 --> 00:03:44,197
Concorde carried about a hundred people.

61
00:03:44,697 --> 00:03:49,317
Per passenger, it burned roughly
three times as much fuel as a 747.

62
00:03:50,251 --> 00:03:53,011
And it brought something
no ordinary airliner makes.

63
00:03:53,711 --> 00:03:54,911
A sonic boom.

64
00:03:58,631 --> 00:04:02,831
A sonic boom isn't a single bang
at the moment you pass the speed of sound.

65
00:04:03,711 --> 00:04:05,671
It follows the plane the whole way,

66
00:04:05,751 --> 00:04:09,491
a carpet of noise on the ground
dozens of kilometres wide.

67
00:04:10,142 --> 00:04:16,042
In 1973, the United States banned
civilian supersonic flight over its land.

68
00:04:16,242 --> 00:04:19,862
And Concorde was kept
mostly to routes over the sea.

69
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:24,320
That left it a handful of routes,
mostly London and Paris to New York.

70
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:26,880
Only twenty were ever built.

71
00:04:27,631 --> 00:04:28,871
And its timing was terrible.

72
00:04:29,101 --> 00:04:33,871
Concorde was designed
in the 1960s, when fuel was cheap.

73
00:04:34,513 --> 00:04:40,093
In early 1973, the big American airlines
that had held options on it pulled out.

74
00:04:40,853 --> 00:04:44,713
Then, that October,
the oil crisis sent fuel prices soaring.

75
00:04:45,373 --> 00:04:47,873
Two separate blows, a few months apart.

76
00:04:48,587 --> 00:04:54,954
In 1997, a return ticket from New York to
London cost over eight thousand dollars.

77
00:04:55,253 --> 00:04:57,193
Some people paid it. Not enough of them.

78
00:04:57,423 --> 00:05:01,593
Concorde had always been expensive
to run and to maintain.

79
00:05:02,477 --> 00:05:05,737
After a crash in 2000,
and a slump in air travel

80
00:05:05,967 --> 00:05:11,497
after September 2001,
it flew its last passengers in 2003.

81
00:05:13,773 --> 00:05:17,893
So now we know where the wall is,
and what it costs to get past it.

82
00:05:18,334 --> 00:05:21,614
But the clearest answer
came in the early 2000s.

83
00:05:21,734 --> 00:05:26,194
Because then, the airlines got something
very close to a direct choice.

84
00:05:26,944 --> 00:05:30,444
In 2001, Boeing proposed
the Sonic Cruiser.

85
00:05:31,324 --> 00:05:33,164
It would fly just under
the speed of sound,

86
00:05:33,394 --> 00:05:35,744
at up to ninety-eight percent of it.

87
00:05:35,824 --> 00:05:40,364
Around fifteen to twenty percent
faster than a normal airliner.

88
00:05:41,188 --> 00:05:46,008
By 2002, Boeing was also discussing
another idea with airlines.

89
00:05:46,948 --> 00:05:48,688
A plane that flew at the usual speed,

90
00:05:48,918 --> 00:05:54,668
but burned seventeen to twenty percent
less fuel than a 767.

91
00:05:55,217 --> 00:05:56,697
Faster... or cheaper.

92
00:05:58,237 --> 00:05:59,457
Cheaper won.

93
00:05:59,537 --> 00:06:04,717
In 2002, Boeing dropped the Sonic Cruiser
and turned to efficiency instead.

94
00:06:05,577 --> 00:06:07,877
That plane became the Boeing 787.

95
00:06:08,790 --> 00:06:11,530
It's the same choice
Whitcomb's wing ran into.

96
00:06:11,890 --> 00:06:16,190
The same trade-off that hurt the Convair,
and that Concorde never escaped.

97
00:06:16,973 --> 00:06:20,293
Again and again,
airlines chose efficiency over speed.

98
00:06:21,459 --> 00:06:24,959
Which makes it sound like airliners
stood still for fifty years.

99
00:06:25,659 --> 00:06:26,859
They didn't.

100
00:06:27,231 --> 00:06:29,471
They just got better at something else.

101
00:06:30,186 --> 00:06:33,586
A new airliner today
uses about half as much fuel per seat,

102
00:06:33,816 --> 00:06:38,226
for the same distance,
as a new one from the 1960s.

103
00:06:38,345 --> 00:06:41,265
And over those years,
flying got much cheaper.

104
00:06:41,982 --> 00:06:45,642
In the United States,
the median domestic return fare,

105
00:06:45,722 --> 00:06:47,302
adjusted for inflation,

106
00:06:47,382 --> 00:06:52,062
fell by almost forty percent
between 1980 and 2005.

107
00:06:52,551 --> 00:06:54,331
And air travel exploded.

108
00:06:55,051 --> 00:06:59,251
In 1970, the world's airlines carried
a few hundred million passengers.

109
00:07:00,151 --> 00:07:03,551
Before the pandemic,
it was four and a half billion a year.

110
00:07:05,351 --> 00:07:07,231
So planes did get faster.

111
00:07:07,311 --> 00:07:09,491
Just not between the airports.

112
00:07:10,399 --> 00:07:15,779
In 1970, flying abroad was still beyond
the budget of many ordinary families.

113
00:07:16,499 --> 00:07:18,739
Over the decades, that changed.

114
00:07:19,159 --> 00:07:22,079
Flying became affordable
to far more people.

115
00:07:23,037 --> 00:07:24,857
We were watching the wrong number.

116
00:07:25,297 --> 00:07:28,397
Speed is the one you can see
on the departures board.

117
00:07:28,897 --> 00:07:32,377
Price is the one that decided
who got on the plane at all.

118
00:07:34,977 --> 00:07:37,337
But that bargain might be about to change.

119
00:07:38,124 --> 00:07:41,924
NASA's X-59 is built to make
a much quieter sonic boom.

120
00:07:42,384 --> 00:07:45,924
Its whole shape is designed
to spread the shock waves out,

121
00:07:46,004 --> 00:07:49,384
so on the ground, it should sound
more like a distant thump.

122
00:07:52,024 --> 00:07:56,304
In 2026, it flew faster than sound
for the first time.

123
00:07:56,556 --> 00:08:01,476
And in 2026, the American regulator
proposed replacing the ban over land

124
00:08:01,706 --> 00:08:04,696
with a limit on how loud the boom
can be on the ground.

125
00:08:05,396 --> 00:08:07,976
It's a proposal, not yet a rule.

126
00:08:08,558 --> 00:08:12,858
A start-up called Boom Supersonic
is designing an airliner for that world,

127
00:08:12,978 --> 00:08:16,278
with a first flight planned
for the late 2020s.

128
00:08:17,297 --> 00:08:19,537
But notice what hasn't changed.

129
00:08:20,017 --> 00:08:22,557
Getting past the wall
still costs more fuel.

130
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:25,180
So the old question is still there.

131
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:27,380
Not whether we can build it.

132
00:08:27,740 --> 00:08:30,020
We could in the 1970s.

133
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:32,380
But who pays for the hours it saves.

134
00:08:33,726 --> 00:08:36,626
For fifty years,
most of us chose the cheaper seat.

135
00:08:36,706 --> 00:08:38,726
And we got what we chose.

136
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:42,480
Planes no faster
than the ones from 1970...

137
00:08:42,560 --> 00:08:46,180
carrying more than ten times
as many passengers a year.

138
00:08:48,310 --> 00:08:50,170
So here's a question for you.

139
00:08:50,670 --> 00:08:53,390
How much would you pay
to land three hours earlier?

140
00:08:53,950 --> 00:08:55,140
Let me know in the comments.
